WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:07.796
So I've been an AI researcher
for over a decade.

00:00:07.796 --> 00:00:11.299
And a couple of months ago,
I got the weirdest email of my career.

00:00:11.925 --> 00:00:13.593
A random stranger wrote to me

00:00:13.635 --> 00:00:17.055
saying that my work in AI
is going to end humanity.

00:00:18.598 --> 00:00:22.352
Now I get it,  AI, it's so hot right now.

00:00:22.352 --> 00:00:23.979
(Laughter)

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.106
It's in the headlines
pretty much every day,

00:00:26.106 --> 00:00:28.024
sometimes because of really cool things

00:00:28.066 --> 00:00:30.235
like discovering new
molecules for medicine

00:00:30.235 --> 00:00:32.487
or that dope Pope
in the white puffer coat.

00:00:33.446 --> 00:00:35.907
But other times the headlines
have been really dark,

00:00:35.907 --> 00:00:39.578
like that chatbot telling that guy
that he should divorce his wife

00:00:39.619 --> 00:00:43.707
or that AI meal planner app
proposing a crowd pleasing recipe

00:00:43.707 --> 00:00:45.709
featuring chlorine gas.

00:00:46.376 --> 00:00:47.794
And in the background,

00:00:47.836 --> 00:00:50.255
we've heard a lot of talk
about doomsday scenarios,

00:00:50.255 --> 00:00:52.173
existential risk and the singularity,

00:00:52.215 --> 00:00:54.718
with letters being written
and events being organized

00:00:54.718 --> 00:00:56.720
to make sure that doesn't happen.

00:00:57.637 --> 00:01:02.267
Now I'm a researcher who studies
AI's impacts on society,

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:05.103
and I don't know what's going
to happen in 10 or 20 years,

00:01:05.145 --> 00:01:07.606
and nobody really does.

00:01:07.981 --> 00:01:12.527
But what I do know is that there's some
pretty nasty things going on right now,

00:01:12.527 --> 00:01:15.405
because AI doesn't exist in a vacuum.

00:01:15.447 --> 00:01:19.367
It is part of society, and it has impacts
on people and the planet.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.662
AI models can contribute
to climate change.

00:01:22.704 --> 00:01:26.166
Their training data uses art
and books created by artists

00:01:26.207 --> 00:01:27.917
and authors without their consent.

00:01:27.959 --> 00:01:31.796
And its deployment can discriminate
against entire communities.

00:01:32.797 --> 00:01:34.924
But we need to start tracking its impacts.

00:01:34.966 --> 00:01:38.553
We need to start being transparent
and disclosing them and creating tools

00:01:38.595 --> 00:01:41.014
so that people understand AI better,

00:01:41.056 --> 00:01:43.391
so that hopefully future
generations of AI models

00:01:43.433 --> 00:01:46.269
are going to be more
trustworthy, sustainable,

00:01:46.269 --> 00:01:49.105
maybe less likely to kill us,
if that's what you're into.

00:01:50.148 --> 00:01:51.900
But let's start with sustainability,

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:57.656
because that cloud that AI models live on
is actually made out of metal, plastic,

00:01:57.656 --> 00:02:00.116
and powered by vast amounts of energy.

00:02:00.116 --> 00:02:04.579
And each time you query an AI model,
it comes with a cost to the planet.

00:02:05.789 --> 00:02:08.833
Last year, I was part
of the BigScience initiative,

00:02:08.833 --> 00:02:10.960
which brought together
a thousand researchers

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.463
from all over the world to create Bloom,

00:02:13.505 --> 00:02:17.842
the first open large language
model, like ChatGPT,

00:02:17.842 --> 00:02:21.388
but with an emphasis on ethics,
transparency and consent.

00:02:21.721 --> 00:02:24.974
And the study I led that looked
at Bloom's environmental impacts

00:02:25.016 --> 00:02:28.269
found that just training it
used as much energy

00:02:28.311 --> 00:02:30.522
as 30 homes in a whole year

00:02:30.563 --> 00:02:32.982
and emitted 25 tons of carbon dioxide,

00:02:33.024 --> 00:02:36.277
which is like driving your car
five times around the planet

00:02:36.319 --> 00:02:39.489
just so somebody can use this model
to tell a knock-knock joke.

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:41.658
And this might not seem like a lot,

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.160
but other similar large language models,

00:02:44.202 --> 00:02:45.328
like GPT-3,

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.914
emit 20 times more carbon.

00:02:47.956 --> 00:02:50.834
But the thing is, tech companies
aren't measuring this stuff.

00:02:50.875 --> 00:02:52.127
They're not disclosing it.

00:02:52.168 --> 00:02:54.629
And so this is probably
only the tip of the iceberg,

00:02:54.629 --> 00:02:56.047
even if it is a melting one.

00:02:56.798 --> 00:03:00.427
And in recent years we've seen
AI models balloon in size

00:03:00.468 --> 00:03:03.930
because the current trend in AI
is "bigger is better."

00:03:04.305 --> 00:03:07.100
But please don't get me started
on why that's the case.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.103
In any case, we've seen large
language models in particular

00:03:10.103 --> 00:03:13.314
grow 2,000 times in size
over the last five years.

00:03:13.314 --> 00:03:16.359
And of course, their environmental
costs are rising as well.

00:03:16.401 --> 00:03:20.196
The most recent work I led,
found that switching out a smaller,

00:03:20.238 --> 00:03:23.575
more efficient model
for a larger language model

00:03:23.616 --> 00:03:27.370
emits 14 times more carbon
for the same task.

00:03:27.412 --> 00:03:29.289
Like telling that knock-knock joke.

00:03:29.289 --> 00:03:32.751
And as we're putting in these models
into cell phones and search engines

00:03:32.792 --> 00:03:35.628
and smart fridges and speakers,

00:03:35.628 --> 00:03:38.256
the environmental costs
are really piling up quickly.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.594
So instead of focusing on some
future existential risks,

00:03:42.635 --> 00:03:45.388
let's talk about current tangible impacts

00:03:45.388 --> 00:03:49.017
and tools we can create to measure
and mitigate these impacts.

00:03:49.893 --> 00:03:51.561
I helped create CodeCarbon,

00:03:51.603 --> 00:03:54.564
a tool that runs in parallel
to AI training code

00:03:54.564 --> 00:03:56.775
that estimates the amount
of energy it consumes

00:03:56.775 --> 00:03:58.443
and the amount of carbon it emits.

00:03:58.485 --> 00:04:01.362
And using a tool like this can help us
make informed choices,

00:04:01.404 --> 00:04:04.657
like choosing one model over the other
because it's more sustainable,

00:04:04.657 --> 00:04:07.577
or deploying AI models
on renewable energy,

00:04:07.619 --> 00:04:10.163
which can drastically reduce
their emissions.

00:04:10.163 --> 00:04:12.248
But let's talk about other things

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:15.251
because there's other impacts of AI
apart from sustainability.

00:04:15.627 --> 00:04:18.755
For example, it's been really
hard for artists and authors

00:04:18.797 --> 00:04:23.009
to prove that their life's work
has been used for training AI models

00:04:23.051 --> 00:04:24.260
without their consent.

00:04:24.302 --> 00:04:27.472
And if you want to sue someone,
you tend to need proof, right?

00:04:27.806 --> 00:04:31.726
So Spawning.ai, an organization
that was founded by artists,

00:04:31.726 --> 00:04:35.063
created this really cool tool
called “Have I Been Trained?”

00:04:35.104 --> 00:04:37.565
And it lets you search
these massive data sets

00:04:37.607 --> 00:04:39.692
to see what they have on you.

00:04:39.734 --> 00:04:41.402
Now, I admit it, I was curious.

00:04:41.444 --> 00:04:43.071
I searched LAION-5B,

00:04:43.112 --> 00:04:45.573
which is this huge data set
of images and text,

00:04:45.615 --> 00:04:48.326
to see if any images of me were in there.

00:04:49.285 --> 00:04:50.870
Now those two first images,

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:53.039
that's me from events I've spoken at.

00:04:53.081 --> 00:04:55.834
But the rest of the images,
none of those are me.

00:04:55.875 --> 00:04:57.877
They're probably of other
women named Sasha

00:04:57.919 --> 00:05:00.547
who put photographs of
themselves up on the internet.

00:05:01.047 --> 00:05:02.674
And this can probably explain why,

00:05:02.715 --> 00:05:04.551
when I query an image generation model

00:05:04.551 --> 00:05:06.845
to generate a photograph
of a woman named Sasha,

00:05:06.886 --> 00:05:09.639
more often than not
I get images of bikini models.

00:05:09.681 --> 00:05:11.307
Sometimes they have two arms,

00:05:11.349 --> 00:05:13.643
sometimes they have three arms,

00:05:13.685 --> 00:05:15.728
but they rarely have any clothes on.

00:05:16.563 --> 00:05:19.357
And while it can be interesting
for people like you and me

00:05:19.357 --> 00:05:21.484
to search these data sets,

00:05:21.526 --> 00:05:23.570
for artists like Karla Ortiz,

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:27.323
this provides crucial evidence
that her life's work, her artwork,

00:05:27.365 --> 00:05:30.326
was used for training AI models
without her consent,

00:05:30.326 --> 00:05:32.662
and she and two artists
used this as evidence

00:05:32.704 --> 00:05:35.498
to file a class action lawsuit
against AI companies

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:37.500
for copyright infringement.

00:05:37.542 --> 00:05:38.710
And most recently --

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:42.088
(Applause)

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:45.174
And most recently Spawning.ai
partnered up with Hugging Face,

00:05:45.216 --> 00:05:46.801
the company where I work at,

00:05:46.801 --> 00:05:51.723
to create opt-in and opt-out mechanisms
for creating these data sets.

00:05:52.098 --> 00:05:55.685
Because artwork created by humans
shouldn’t be an all-you-can-eat buffet

00:05:55.727 --> 00:05:57.520
for training AI language models.

00:05:58.313 --> 00:06:02.567
(Applause)

00:06:02.567 --> 00:06:04.903
The very last thing I want
to talk about is bias.

00:06:04.944 --> 00:06:06.863
You probably hear about this a lot.

00:06:07.196 --> 00:06:10.909
Formally speaking, it's when AI models
encode patterns and beliefs

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:14.078
that can represent stereotypes
or racism and sexism.

00:06:14.412 --> 00:06:17.624
One of my heroes, Dr. Joy Buolamwini,
experienced this firsthand

00:06:17.665 --> 00:06:20.710
when she realized that AI systems
wouldn't even detect her face

00:06:20.752 --> 00:06:22.921
unless she was wearing
a white-colored mask.

00:06:22.962 --> 00:06:26.716
Digging deeper, she found
that common facial recognition systems

00:06:26.758 --> 00:06:30.011
were vastly worse for women of color
compared to white men.

00:06:30.428 --> 00:06:35.725
And when biased models like this
are deployed in law enforcement settings,

00:06:35.767 --> 00:06:40.063
this can result in false accusations,
even wrongful imprisonment,

00:06:40.063 --> 00:06:43.983
which we've seen happen
to multiple people in recent months.

00:06:44.025 --> 00:06:47.111
For example, Porcha Woodruff
was wrongfully accused of carjacking

00:06:47.111 --> 00:06:48.363
at eight months pregnant

00:06:48.363 --> 00:06:51.324
because an AI system
wrongfully identified her.

00:06:52.325 --> 00:06:54.327
But sadly, these systems are black boxes,

00:06:54.369 --> 00:07:00.333
and even their creators can't say exactly
why they work the way they do.

00:07:00.917 --> 00:07:04.379
And for example, for image
generation systems,

00:07:04.379 --> 00:07:08.508
if they're used in contexts
like generating a forensic sketch

00:07:08.549 --> 00:07:11.260
based on a description of a perpetrator,

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:14.847
they take all those biases
and they spit them back out

00:07:14.889 --> 00:07:18.351
for terms like dangerous criminal,
terrorists or gang member,

00:07:18.393 --> 00:07:20.561
which of course is super dangerous

00:07:20.603 --> 00:07:25.024
when these tools are deployed in society.

00:07:25.566 --> 00:07:27.860
And so in order to understand
these tools better,

00:07:27.902 --> 00:07:31.114
I created this tool called
the Stable Bias Explorer,

00:07:31.155 --> 00:07:34.534
which lets you explore the bias
of image generation models

00:07:34.575 --> 00:07:36.244
through the lens of professions.

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:40.415
So try to picture
a scientist in your mind.

00:07:40.456 --> 00:07:41.624
Don't look at me.

00:07:41.666 --> 00:07:43.001
What do you see?

00:07:43.835 --> 00:07:45.336
A lot of the same thing, right?

00:07:45.378 --> 00:07:47.755
Men in glasses and lab coats.

00:07:47.797 --> 00:07:49.507
And none of them look like me.

00:07:50.174 --> 00:07:51.634
And the thing is,

00:07:51.676 --> 00:07:54.929
is that we looked at all these
different image generation models

00:07:54.929 --> 00:07:56.556
and found a lot of the same thing:

00:07:56.597 --> 00:07:59.183
significant representation
of whiteness and masculinity

00:07:59.225 --> 00:08:01.352
across all 150 professions
that we looked at,

00:08:01.352 --> 00:08:03.146
even if compared to the real world,

00:08:03.187 --> 00:08:05.023
the US Labor Bureau of Statistics.

00:08:05.023 --> 00:08:08.067
These models show lawyers as men,

00:08:08.109 --> 00:08:11.571
and CEOs as men,
almost 100 percent of the time,

00:08:11.571 --> 00:08:14.741
even though we all know
not all of them are white and male.

00:08:14.782 --> 00:08:19.162
And sadly, my tool hasn't been used
to write legislation yet.

00:08:19.203 --> 00:08:23.166
But I recently presented it
at a UN event about gender bias

00:08:23.166 --> 00:08:27.045
as an example of how we can make tools
for people from all walks of life,

00:08:27.086 --> 00:08:29.338
even those who don't know how to code,

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:32.633
to engage with and better understand AI
because we use professions,

00:08:32.633 --> 00:08:35.720
but you can use any terms
that are of interest to you.

00:08:36.596 --> 00:08:39.348
And as these models are being deployed,

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:42.518
are being woven into the very
fabric of our societies,

00:08:42.518 --> 00:08:44.562
our cell phones, our social media feeds,

00:08:44.604 --> 00:08:47.815
even our justice systems
and our economies have AI in them.

00:08:47.815 --> 00:08:51.694
And it's really important
that AI stays accessible

00:08:51.736 --> 00:08:56.449
so that we know both how it works
and when it doesn't work.

00:08:56.908 --> 00:09:01.204
And there's no single solution
for really complex things like bias

00:09:01.245 --> 00:09:03.664
or copyright or climate change.

00:09:03.664 --> 00:09:06.375
But by creating tools
to measure AI's impact,

00:09:06.375 --> 00:09:09.712
we can start getting an idea
of how bad they are

00:09:09.754 --> 00:09:12.256
and start addressing them as we go.

00:09:12.256 --> 00:09:15.593
Start creating guardrails
to protect society and the planet.

00:09:16.177 --> 00:09:18.513
And once we have this information,

00:09:18.513 --> 00:09:20.348
companies can use it in order to say,

00:09:20.389 --> 00:09:23.559
OK, we're going to choose this model
because it's more sustainable,

00:09:23.601 --> 00:09:25.645
this model because it respects copyright.

00:09:25.686 --> 00:09:28.773
Legislators who really need
information to write laws,

00:09:28.773 --> 00:09:32.235
can use these tools to develop
new regulation mechanisms

00:09:32.276 --> 00:09:36.072
or governance for AI
as it gets deployed into society.

00:09:36.114 --> 00:09:38.491
And users like you and me
can use this information

00:09:38.491 --> 00:09:41.828
to choose AI models that we can trust,

00:09:41.869 --> 00:09:44.789
not to misrepresent us
and not to misuse our data.

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:47.708
But what did I reply to that email

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:50.711
that said that my work
is going to destroy humanity?

00:09:50.711 --> 00:09:54.757
I said that focusing
on AI's future existential risks

00:09:54.799 --> 00:09:56.843
is a distraction from its current,

00:09:56.843 --> 00:09:58.678
very tangible impacts

00:09:58.719 --> 00:10:02.723
and the work we should be doing
right now, or even yesterday,

00:10:02.723 --> 00:10:04.642
for reducing these impacts.

00:10:04.684 --> 00:10:08.729
Because yes, AI is moving quickly,
but it's not a done deal.

00:10:08.771 --> 00:10:11.274
We're building the road as we walk it,

00:10:11.274 --> 00:10:15.069
and we can collectively decide
what direction we want to go in together.

00:10:15.069 --> 00:10:16.279
Thank you.

00:10:16.279 --> 00:10:18.281
(Applause)

